file: add support for HTTP Range requests

Serve single byte ranges from static files so that clients can resume
interrupted downloads and seek within media files.

The Range header is parsed for GET requests and a 206 Partial Content
response with a Content-Range header is returned when the requested
range is satisfiable.  Suffix ranges ("bytes=-N") and open ended ranges
("bytes=N-") are supported, the last byte position is clamped to the
end of the file and multi-range requests are ignored by serving the
complete representation, as permitted by RFC 9110.

Requests for an unsatisfiable range are answered with 416 Range Not
Satisfiable and a Content-Range header describing the current length.
Accept-Ranges: bytes is now advertised for all static files.

If-Range is evaluated properly: a matching strong entity tag or an
exact Last-Modified date keeps the range, anything else disables it and
the full file is sent instead of unconditionally failing with 412.
